<details open><summary>1. The core rule: Arabic cannot start on a sukuun</summary>
<p>Arabic syllables open with one consonant plus a vowel (CV). A word whose first real letter is silent (sukuun) would begin a consonant cluster, which the language does not allow. The Helper Alif is inserted at the start as a <b>prothetic vowel</b> (a temporary vowel added before the word). When another word comes before it, the helper is <b>elided</b> and the earlier sound flows straight into the sukuun.</p>
<table><tr><th></th><th class="hv">Wasl (Helper) ٱ</th><th class="qt">Qat' (Boss) أ إ</th></tr>
<tr><th>Behaviour</th><td>Disappears when connected</td><td>Always pronounced (glottal stop /ʔ/)</td></tr>
<tr><th>Position</th><td>Only at the start of a word</td><td>Start, middle or end</td></tr>
<tr><th>Examples</th><td><span class="ar">اسْتَغْفَرَ، الْكِتَابِ</span><br><span class="mut">he sought forgiveness; the book</span></td><td><span class="ar">أَحْمَدُ، يَأْكُلُ</span><br><span class="mut">Ahmad; he eats</span></td></tr></table></details>
<details><summary>2. Particles: only "al-" (ال) gets the Helper, always with fatha (a)</summary>
<p>The helper carries the silent laam. Moon letters keep the laam (<span class="ar">ٱلْقَمَر</span> al-qamar, “the moon”); sun letters assimilate it into a doubled sound (<span class="ar">ٱلشَّمْس</span> ash-shams, “the sun”). Connected: <span class="ar">وَٱلْقَمَر</span> wa-lqamar (“and the moon”), <span class="ar">وَٱلشَّمْس</span> wash-shams (“and the sun”).</p></details>
<details><summary>3. Nouns: Kasra (i) by default, a closed list</summary>
<p>The famous seven (kasra, because kasra is the default vowel for nouns lacking "al-"); the same holds for verbal nouns of the longer verb forms, e.g. <span class="ar">اسْتِغْفَار</span> (istighfār, “seeking forgiveness”).</p>
<table id="n7"></table></details>
<details><summary>4. Verbs: the 1-2-3 Radar Rule (commands)</summary>
<p>Letter 1 is the Helper, letter 2 is silent, letter 3 is the target. <b>3rd letter has damma → helper takes damma. 3rd letter has fatha or kasra → helper takes kasra.</b> Beware the four fake dammas below. Longer verbs such as <span class="ar">اسْتَغْفِرْ</span> (“seek forgiveness!”) follow the same radar and give kasra.</p>
<div id="rad"></div></details>
<details><summary>5. The Ninja Warning: four fake dammas</summary>
<p>When the 3rd letter shows damma only to match a plural <span class="ar">و</span>, the original vowel was kasra, so the helper stays kasra.</p>
<table id="fk"></table></details>
